  "slug": "cmpcbs-order-quantity-derivative",
  "title": "One giant order a year to save on ordering?",
  "screen": {
    "type": "data",
    "level": 1,
    "data": {
      "workspace": "order_plan.xlsx, flour purchasing, Hearthside Bakery Cooperative (fictional)",
      "flags": [
        {
          "id": "ask",
          "label": "Operations manager, Lena Fischer",
          "text": "Each order costs us $150 in fees and handling, so I want one order of 12,000 bags a year. The supplier needs our order size by 12:00.",
          "when": "true",
          "severity": "info"
        },
        {
          "id": "seen",
          "label": "Pattern",
          "text": "The plan minimizes one cost and ignores the other.",
          "when": "not isNull(lookAt) and not fixed",
          "severity": "warning"
        },
        {
          "id": "lock",
          "label": "Supplier",
          "text": "12:00: the order size went to the supplier as it stood.",
          "when": "not isNull(lockedAt)",
          "severity": "error"
        }
      ],
      "charts": [],
      "tables": [],
      "code": [
        {
          "id": "code",
          "label": "order_plan.xlsx",
          "language": "text",
          "lines": [
            {
              "id": "c0",
              "text": "Q = 12,000 (one order: ordering cost 150)",
              "when": "fix == \"bug\"",
              "mark": "not isNull(lookAt) ? \"error\" : \"none\""
            },
            {
              "id": "c1",
              "text": "C'(Q) = -1,800,000 / Q^2 + 2 = 0  ->  Q* = sqrt(900,000) = 949; C''(Q) = 3,600,000 / Q^3 > 0 (a minimum)",
              "when": "fix == \"deriv\"",
              "mark": "\"changed\""
            },
            {
              "id": "c2",
              "text": "Try Q = 500, 1,000 and 2,000 in the sheet; pick the cheapest (1,000)",
              "when": "fix == \"grid\"",
              "mark": "\"warning\""
            },
            {
              "id": "c3",
              "text": "Q* = sqrt(D S / H) = sqrt(450,000) = 671",
              "when": "fix == \"half\"",
              "mark": "\"warning\""
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "meters": [
        {
          "id": "due",
          "label": "Supplier",
          "value": "12:00"
        }
      ]
    }
  },
  "objective": "Applications of derivatives, including L'Hopital's Rule",
  "secondaryObjectives": [
    "The derivative: formal definition and differentiation rules"
  ],
  "sources": [
    {
      "cite": "OpenStax, Calculus Volume 1, 4.7 Applied Optimization Problems",
      "checkedAt": "2026-11-17",
      "url": "https://openstax.org/books/calculus-volume-1/pages/4-7-applied-optimization-problems",
      "note": "Read 2026-11-17 through a summarizing fetch. The strategy ends: 'Locate the maximum or minimum value of the function', which 'typically involves looking for critical points and evaluating a function at endpoints'; the examples compare the critical point's value with the endpoints. The order-quantity cost function and its derivative are the author's application."
    },
    {
      "cite": "Author's scenario (REQ-333)",
      "checkedAt": "2026-11-17",
      "note": "Every organization, person, count and deadline is invented teaching data; tool output is an authored rendering in the tool style."
    }
  ],
  "brief": {
    "situation": "You help plan purchasing at Hearthside Bakery Cooperative (fictional). Annual demand is 12,000 bags of flour, each order costs $150, and holding a bag for a year costs $4. The manager proposes one order of 12,000 to cut ordering costs. The supplier needs the order size by 12:00. It is 11:05.",
    "handover": [
      "Total yearly cost C(Q) = 12,000 x 150 / Q + 4 Q / 2 for order size Q (bags)."
    ],
    "howItWorks": [
      "Pick a step from the action list: look, change, check or ship. Nothing you pick is typed or executed: every step shows an authored result.",
      "Each step takes working minutes, and the clock moves by the minutes it takes. The deadline runs on the same clock.",
      "Shipping is final for this case. Afterwards you record what you found and explain your reasoning."
    ]
  },
  "clock": {
    "start": "11:05",
    "tick": "1min",
    "maxDuration": "150min",
    "calendar": "clock"
  },
  "params": {
